WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.970
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.565
URBAN: I'm Glen Urban,
Dean of the MIT Sloan

00:00:15.565 --> 00:00:16.440
School of Management.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.240
And it's my privilege
to welcome you

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.390
to this fifth lecture
in our industry leaders

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.790
in technology management series.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.130
This is co-sponsored by
the School of Management

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.870
and the School of Engineering.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:31.260
We bring to campus here
the world's leading CEOs

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:34.140
to give us their
guidance and wisdom.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.860
You may have been here last
week when Jack Smith was here

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.060
from GM.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.670
You probably also remember
our earlier speakers

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:45.810
Alex Troutman from Ford and
Gordon Binder from Amgen.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.540
Today it gives me great
pleasure to introduce

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.780
an equally eminent
speaker, Dr. Andy

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:56.160
Grove, who is the president
and CEO of Intel Corporation.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.670
Dr. Grove's life really
shows graphically

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.200
the opportunities in America.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.519
He came here from Hungary in
1957, enrolled in City College

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:08.880
and got a bachelor's degree in
chemical engineering in 1960.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.700
Went on to get a PhD the
University of California

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.350
in 1963.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:18.750
And in 1968, teamed up with
Gordon Moore and Bob Noyce,

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.970
then at Fairchild
Semiconductor, and they

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.160
formed some little
startup company called

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:29.250
Intel, which has been the
home for Andy for 30 years,

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.410
at this point.

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:32.370
Tremendous career.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:34.182
A tremendous company.

00:01:34.182 --> 00:01:35.640
He's been one of
the driving forces

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.340
behind making this successful.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.230
You may recall that
five years ago,

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.570
or so, various people
were predicting

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:47.020
that the US IC industry
would be out of business.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:51.810
Well, in the last years, 1990
to 95, under Andy's leadership

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.170
the sales at Intel have gone
from $4 billion to $16 billion,

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:58.680
the profits from $650
million to $3.5 billion,

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:02.710
and the stock price on
average from $10 to over $65.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:05.880
So it's hard to find a
more successful track

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:09.330
record for company leadership.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.460
Dr. Grove is going
to speak to us today

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.610
on exploiting the crisis points
challenging every company

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.110
and career.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.300
And you don't have to
take too many notes

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:22.400
because he's just
written a new book that

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.710
describes many of these ideas.

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:28.250
Only the paranoid survive.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:32.330
And if you happen to see
the latest Forbes magazine,

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.700
Forbes says that,
"this is probably

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.370
the best book written on
business by a business person

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:41.330
since Alfred Sloan's My
Years at General Motors."

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:45.180
So, welcome home and you're
in good company here.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:49.350
Please welcome me in joining
with Dr. Grove for this

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:49.850
[INAUDIBLE].

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:52.832
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:02.973
GROVE: First of all,
is the mic working?

00:03:02.973 --> 00:03:03.820
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:05.490
GROVE: Good.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.230
What I would like to
do is, first of all

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.800
stay nearby here if that's
OK with your protocol.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:16.590
And use this overhead
projector to walk you

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:19.530
through some of the
observations that

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.380
are in fact, the
kernel of that book,

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:28.380
and have been based on my
experience and laboratory

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:33.810
tested, beta tested if you wish,
on the competitive business

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:38.250
school whose name
shall remain unnamed.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:40.850
But it's on the west
coast near where I live.

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:43.305
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:48.320
How many of you are business
school students here?

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:53.665
How many are engineers?

00:03:53.665 --> 00:03:57.397
Now, how many of you are
business school students who--

00:03:57.397 --> 00:03:59.230
how many of you have
an undergraduate degree

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:01.300
in engineering?

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:02.370
That's pretty good.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:03.573
OK.

00:04:03.573 --> 00:04:04.990
The reason I'm
asking is because I

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:07.630
want to tell you what the
real title of this book

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:11.140
was intended to be, and I
didn't want to miss my mark.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:16.130
I wanted to call this book,
strategic inflection points.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:22.590
And it's meant for
general purpose audiences,

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:25.400
and my publisher
wouldn't hear of it.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:30.830
And to make a long story
short, a long and short of it,

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:32.350
it got a catchier title.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:35.020
But that is what
the book is about.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:36.610
Strategic inflection
point is my way

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:41.980
of visualizing and describing
a fundamental change

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:46.690
in the well-being of a business.

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:49.760
Fundamental change in the
well-being of an industry.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:54.750
It's a curve more or less going
like this, which I don't know,

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:56.740
can the overflow
people see this?

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:00.890
There is a video set up for it?

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:01.390
OK.

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:08.020
The hardest time we
all have at business

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:12.013
is not a break point in
a curve, if the curve

00:05:12.013 --> 00:05:14.180
represents the well-being
of the flow of a business.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:18.130
It is exactly at
that subtle point

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:20.140
where the curvature
changes, we're

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:22.390
going from convex to concave,
or the other way around.

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:25.630
I never could figure that out.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:30.310
And it's exactly at that point
that the nature of the business

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:31.600
around us is changing.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:33.110
And it's changing
in a subtle way,

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:36.250
but changing in a profound
and lasting fashion.

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:37.930
And those people
in a business who

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:42.400
do not make the adjustment
to the new curvature

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:43.900
go into decline.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:46.630
That's kind of the
graphical representation

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:49.510
of what I'm talking about.

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:52.810
And another way
to look at this is

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:58.230
to look at the conditions under
which the businesses undergo,

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:01.190
or businesses in general,
undergo strategic inflection

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:02.270
points.

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:07.870
And I trust the business
school students among you

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:09.970
have studied, in
one form or another,

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:11.470
camouflaged as it
might have been,

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:15.370
Porter's competitive
strategy concept.

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:17.440
Probably called five
forces diagram or something

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:19.480
to take the brand name of it.

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:20.690
Is that right?

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:21.190
Yes.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:21.800
OK.

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:24.130
That's what they do at
the other named school.

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:24.940
Anyway.

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:29.170
Porter made a real contribution
by basically graphically

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:31.300
depicting competitive forces.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:34.150
And I redrew it a little
bit because I amended it

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:37.270
with another contribution
that actually

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:40.300
came from a couple of
professors, one at Harvard one

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:41.610
at Yale.

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:46.120
The six forces to
broaden it, that affect

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:50.950
a business: customers
power, competitive forces,

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:54.490
intra-industry competitive
forces, supplier power, forces

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:57.550
of substitution, and adding
to that complementors.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:02.140
People who are crucial in
adding additional value

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:07.120
to the value of the
product in question.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:10.750
Easiest way to look at it,
Microsoft is our complementor.

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:14.590
Microsoft software makes Intel
microprocessors more valuable.

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:16.660
Intel is Microsoft's
complementor.

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:19.590
Microsoft software, without
Intel microprocessors,

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:22.900
would clearly not be as
valuable as it is with.

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:25.750
This is sort of the general
analysis of describing

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:28.928
how a business operates
and this is not

00:07:28.928 --> 00:07:30.220
what I was going to talk about.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:31.530
What we are going
to look at and what

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:33.030
we are going to
examine here is what

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:38.920
happens when one of those forces
becomes very, very much larger

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:40.270
than the others.

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:47.500
And I want to call it an order
of magnitude in order to--

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:49.500
order of magnitude
is not exactly

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:51.790
a trade publication term.

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:53.080
I called it a 10x force.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:57.497
A 10 for 10 force, 10
times increase relative

00:07:57.497 --> 00:07:58.330
to the other forces.

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:00.820
What happens when
there is such, one

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:04.360
of the forces that determine
the well-being of a business

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:06.700
really grows out
of bound and so big

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:09.820
that it can distort the picture.

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:11.200
And what tends to
happen at times

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:16.540
like that is that the very
nature of the industry changes.

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:18.910
Not the business, not the
competitive well-being

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:23.120
of the business alone, but the
whole business model changes.

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:26.030
And I give you an
example that comes

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:29.330
right from our own
industry on this one.

00:08:29.330 --> 00:08:31.690
And on the left
of the diagram you

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:35.650
would have maybe the business
model of the computing industry

00:08:35.650 --> 00:08:40.600
as it used to be performed
in the '60s, '70s, '80s.

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:46.900
Computers were sold as
vertically integrated devices,

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:49.842
proprietary platforms,
proprietary operating systems,

00:08:49.842 --> 00:08:52.300
applications developed for that
propriety operating system,

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:57.070
and a whole stack of things
sold by Salesforce that

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:00.670
was unique to that enterprise.

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:03.070
Then came the personal
computer, which

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:06.790
was in fact a genuine almost
quantitatively correctly

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:10.493
describable 10x force in
that its cost effectiveness

00:09:10.493 --> 00:09:12.160
relative to mainframes
or mini computers

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:16.000
was about 10-fold right
from the inception on.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:18.460
And it was clearly a toy.

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:20.230
And it was clearly unreliable.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:22.540
And it couldn't do
the software that

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:24.340
was pertinent to
business processes.

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:27.310
But it was so cost
effective that

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:29.250
is represented a 10x force.

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:32.410
And in front of our
eyes in the course

00:09:32.410 --> 00:09:35.590
of the decade of the
'80s, the entire structure

00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:39.670
of the industry changed from
the vertical towers of computer

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:41.500
products competing
with each other

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:45.160
to a completely horizontal
industry in which

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:47.950
microprocessors compete
with other microprocessors,

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:52.240
operating systems compete
with other operating systems,

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:53.920
applications
software in a packet

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:56.770
form like a bunch
of commodity goods,

00:09:56.770 --> 00:09:58.380
like a bunch of
detergents, compete

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:01.800
for deck shelf space with
other application software.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:06.210
And direct sales goes out
as a means of distribution.

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:08.310
Distribution becomes
all indirect.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:14.190
In terms of your experience
it's very difficult

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:18.570
to imagine how ridiculous
the whole concept of selling

00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:22.733
computers through a
telephone and through mail

00:10:22.733 --> 00:10:24.150
would have sounded
to somebody who

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:27.210
was steeped in the computer
industry of the left side

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:27.960
of the diagram.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:32.820
I mean it was, it
wasn't even laughable.

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:35.820
People wouldn't even
engage that idea.

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:37.070
So that's how much it changed.

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:39.450
So the business model
changed under this part

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:41.668
of this 10x force
from a particular way

00:10:41.668 --> 00:10:43.710
of doing computing to a
completely different view

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:45.060
in computing.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:49.170
And the time period that I'm
interested in talking about,

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:50.890
and I'm interested
in considering,

00:10:50.890 --> 00:10:55.170
is what happens in between
those two by two diagrams.

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:57.180
I call it the valley of death.

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:59.010
That's one way to describe it.

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:02.700
Another way to describe
it is, picture some kind

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:05.350
of a graphical
figure being morphed

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:06.600
into another graphical figure.

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:08.790
A phase being morphed
into another phase.

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:11.520
And it's in-between when
the bits are all distorted

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:14.010
and all jumbled up, when you
can't make heads and tails out

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:17.820
of the diagram, there's
no shape to things.

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:21.720
That is when managing
an enterprise,

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:24.480
and for that matter
managing a career,

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:26.050
are the trickiest tasks.

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:27.750
And that's what we
are talking about.

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:29.708
Let me give you a couple
more examples to bring

00:11:29.708 --> 00:11:32.410
some reality to this.

00:11:32.410 --> 00:11:37.270
And the 10x force can
come in a variety of ways.

00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:39.420
I described in the case
of the computer industry,

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:41.930
it came through technology.

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:45.000
Higher power technology
brought a great deal

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:47.520
of cost effectiveness.

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:49.260
In the case of the
Porter diagram,

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:52.625
it caused substitution
in a very large scale.

00:11:55.550 --> 00:11:57.590
It can come in
various other ways.

00:11:57.590 --> 00:12:01.920
If you are a retailer in
a small or medium town

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:05.970
someplace in America, the
strategic inflection point

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:08.040
arrives in the form of
a bunch of surveyors

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:11.760
who come and start surveying
the property in one

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:15.210
of the most highly traveled
intersections in town.

00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:20.820
And then a sign goes up,
future home of Walmart.

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:24.900
Now you say, what's
the difference?

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:28.330
There never was just one
retail store in this town.

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:30.030
There were always
a bunch of them.

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:31.550
Any owner of a
retail store would

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:33.420
say I always had competition.

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:34.380
I have competition now.

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:37.230
So one more, what the
hell is the difference?

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:38.580
And this is the 10x force.

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:41.370
Walmart is not just
one more competitor.

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:46.920
It is the largest retailer
in the world tuned, tuned,

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:50.430
genetically tuned, to
operate at thin margins, very

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:53.250
high volumes, using information
technology, satellite

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:57.510
technologies, transportation
technologies, all fine tuned

00:12:57.510 --> 00:12:59.760
to do that to a precision.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:04.500
That small town retailer
cannot conduct their business,

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:07.065
its business, in the same
fashion just thinking that

00:13:07.065 --> 00:13:11.160
it's another competitor, or else
they're going to go downhill

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:12.490
on that curve.

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:14.940
So that's the example
of a competitive force

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:17.190
that would be a 10x force.

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:22.710
Sometimes these forces come in
the form of regulatory changes.

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:25.310
An example of that is
what happened in 1984

00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:30.580
when in the form of the
modified final judgment,

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:35.490
judge brought about the
deregulation of a many decade

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:43.190
old, countrywide monopoly that
was the AT&amp;T of that time.

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:46.560
And the repercussions of that
change have been immense.

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:52.330
And it impacted the
well-being of not one company,

00:13:52.330 --> 00:13:53.350
but a lot of companies.

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:56.670
They certainly impacted
the well-being of AT&amp;T,

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:57.900
old and new.

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:00.120
Impacted the well-being
of MCI, Sprint,

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:03.120
and untold alternative
service providers.

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:07.200
Impacted the well-being of the
regional operating companies.

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:10.800
Impacted the well-being
of customers.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:13.830
And most importantly,
impacted the well-being

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:19.920
of probably 150,000 employees
of the old AT&amp;T, who

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:23.320
are no longer working for
any telephone company.

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:24.600
So this is not a change.

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:26.940
This is a cataclysmic change.

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:28.890
It was a strategic
inflection point.

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:32.400
And very few people
realized at the time,

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:35.040
I was around at the time,
I was calling on AT&amp;T

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:36.630
because it's one
of our customers.

00:14:36.630 --> 00:14:37.750
They were confused.

00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:40.760
It was like the bits in the
middle of the morphing process.

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:42.510
They didn't know what
was going to happen.

00:14:42.510 --> 00:14:45.300
But none of them could
foresee the extent

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:47.080
it was going to change
their well-being

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:50.977
and their business processes.

00:14:50.977 --> 00:14:52.560
We talked about the
computer industry.

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:54.310
And the one other thing
that I want to say

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:58.370
is, which we are in the
middle of, the internet.

00:14:58.370 --> 00:15:01.600
You know, you pick up any
trade magazine, any newspaper,

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:03.350
any anything, I mean
the internet is being

00:15:03.350 --> 00:15:06.470
beaten to death in the press.

00:15:06.470 --> 00:15:10.460
But the larger picture is that
there is a phenomenon coming

00:15:10.460 --> 00:15:16.700
here that is likely to
affect not just one industry,

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:21.290
like all of these examples have,
but half a dozen industries.

00:15:21.290 --> 00:15:25.390
Industries that don't
even know that this--

00:15:25.390 --> 00:15:26.960
what, how does
packet switching have

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:29.600
to do with the media industry?

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:32.120
What the hell does
the internet has

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:35.383
to do with the entertainment
industry in Hollywood?

00:15:35.383 --> 00:15:37.550
What does it have to do
with the service industries?

00:15:37.550 --> 00:15:41.780
And yet, enormous
industries that

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:43.610
have kind of minded
their own business,

00:15:43.610 --> 00:15:47.720
never even until a few years
ago even have heard the phrase,

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:49.335
will be morphed.

00:15:49.335 --> 00:15:50.210
Will be restructured.

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:53.480
Will have to undergo over
the next several years

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:56.540
a valley of death type
of transformation.

00:15:56.540 --> 00:15:58.730
Now exactly how
will that shape up?

00:15:58.730 --> 00:15:59.360
I don't know.

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:01.430
In 10 years I could come
back if your interested,

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:03.848
and I'll tell you
exactly how it happened.

00:16:03.848 --> 00:16:06.140
Just like I can't tell you
about the computer industry.

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:09.880
But right now, it's a bunch
of bits in a confused fashion,

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:13.670
and we are just entering
the valley of death.

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:16.010
It is very confusing
in the middle of it.

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:17.000
That's not unusual.

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:21.110
That is what it's like because
the new business model doesn't

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:22.800
take shape very clearly.

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:25.040
And a lot of people
have different ideas

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:26.660
on how it's going to be.

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:28.880
And which is why, of course,
all the newspapers are

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:32.270
filled with very
superficial speculation

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:35.990
on the shape of the future.

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:39.880
So to sum this, the
strategic inflection point

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:41.530
happens when there's
a major change

00:16:41.530 --> 00:16:43.135
in the competitive environment.

00:16:43.135 --> 00:16:47.230
It can be technological,
regulation, 10x competition

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:48.850
force.

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:55.950
And a key element is
that it is very often,

00:16:55.950 --> 00:16:57.858
and I don't know if
always, because I haven't

00:16:57.858 --> 00:16:59.150
made an exhaustive study of it.

00:16:59.150 --> 00:17:01.690
But very often, the
first signs of it,

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:04.060
the first recognition
of it, does not

00:17:04.060 --> 00:17:08.290
come from senior
management, people like me.

00:17:08.290 --> 00:17:11.349
But come from people who
are in the front lines

00:17:11.349 --> 00:17:16.630
of the key activities
of the corporation.

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:19.579
Salesforce is very
likely to realize

00:17:19.579 --> 00:17:23.960
changing customer preferences
much before sales management

00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:24.900
is.

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:27.210
Now, do they understand that
it's a change of customer

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:27.710
preferences?

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:31.130
No, they just understand
something that used to work

00:17:31.130 --> 00:17:32.367
doesn't work anymore.

00:17:32.367 --> 00:17:33.950
Then they go home
and tell that story.

00:17:33.950 --> 00:17:37.160
And they can't make a
coherent story out of it.

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:38.370
So they get thrown out.

00:17:38.370 --> 00:17:38.880
Go away.

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:40.700
Just work harder.

00:17:40.700 --> 00:17:43.700
And the fact of the matter
is something changed in them.

00:17:43.700 --> 00:17:48.630
And because it is so murky,
they can't articulate the story.

00:17:48.630 --> 00:17:50.730
And the internet is
a perfect example.

00:17:50.730 --> 00:17:52.880
The people who
understood those things,

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:55.010
that were people who were
users of the internet.

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:57.300
But they didn't understand
how big it was going to be.

00:17:57.300 --> 00:18:00.410
Or they understood that their
little world was suddenly

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:02.540
intruded by a bunch of
people who had no business

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:05.290
being on the internet.

00:18:05.290 --> 00:18:09.500
And I see a number
of nodding heads.

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:12.860
And those people
probably realize

00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:15.590
that something is
changing in the aggregate

00:18:15.590 --> 00:18:19.160
a long time before most
people in corporate

00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:22.940
may even had a whiff of it.

00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:25.460
What is going to happen
when that happens

00:18:25.460 --> 00:18:29.960
is that individuals
like that are

00:18:29.960 --> 00:18:33.500
going to start turning
into being Cassandras.

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:37.030
And because mostly this
change is not for the better.

00:18:37.030 --> 00:18:40.700
Mostly the change is something
that worked, doesn't work.

00:18:40.700 --> 00:18:47.210
And our products are no
longer [INAUDIBLE] at them.

00:18:47.210 --> 00:18:52.610
In our case, when Intel was a
semiconductor memory company.

00:18:52.610 --> 00:18:54.413
We pioneered
semiconductor memories.

00:18:54.413 --> 00:18:56.330
We were the leaders in
semiconductor memories.

00:18:56.330 --> 00:19:01.460
In the mid '80s, the Japanese
came and became a 10x force

00:19:01.460 --> 00:19:04.370
for us in
semiconductor memories.

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:07.970
And one of the first
signs as I look back,

00:19:07.970 --> 00:19:10.750
I didn't look at it at
that time as a sign.

00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:12.900
I looked at it as a
whining sales force.

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:16.280
But one of the first
signs that I look back

00:19:16.280 --> 00:19:20.090
that should have tipped
us off was our sales force

00:19:20.090 --> 00:19:24.140
in Japan saying that
the customers are not

00:19:24.140 --> 00:19:29.060
as respectful of them
as they used to be.

00:19:29.060 --> 00:19:32.012
And that was such a telling
story in retrospect.

00:19:32.012 --> 00:19:33.470
If I look back on
the curve, that's

00:19:33.470 --> 00:19:36.200
when the curve was beginning
to enter into the inflection

00:19:36.200 --> 00:19:36.920
point.

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:41.190
There weren't because they knew
that their internal capability,

00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:43.590
the customer's internal
capability, the semiconductor

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:48.210
sister division was going
to run circles around us.

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:49.040
They knew that.

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:51.230
And that attitude changed
toward our sales force.

00:19:51.230 --> 00:19:54.140
Or sales force told us.

00:19:54.140 --> 00:19:57.790
Us, being management,
ignored it.

00:19:57.790 --> 00:19:59.180
We ignored it.

00:19:59.180 --> 00:20:02.420
When they showed up
here, we ignored it

00:20:02.420 --> 00:20:04.340
as we were starting
to lose market share.

00:20:04.340 --> 00:20:07.190
And the people in our company
who realized what was going on

00:20:07.190 --> 00:20:11.420
was our group of
production planners

00:20:11.420 --> 00:20:14.510
who started adjusting
our factory loading away

00:20:14.510 --> 00:20:16.520
from memories, toward
microprocessors.

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:17.390
Why?

00:20:17.390 --> 00:20:18.740
Because we had better
business opportunities

00:20:18.740 --> 00:20:19.550
in microprocessors.

00:20:19.550 --> 00:20:21.008
We made more money
microprocessors.

00:20:21.008 --> 00:20:25.100
So we're not making a big
fanfare [INAUDIBLE] lots

00:20:25.100 --> 00:20:26.020
by [INAUDIBLE] lots.

00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:27.980
They just move the
production lots

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:30.450
from memories to
microprocessors.

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:33.980
So by the time those of us
who ran the company started

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:38.360
facing up to that, that our core
business, which were memories,

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:41.660
was going away, because of
these incremental our actions

00:20:41.660 --> 00:20:44.000
of our finance people,
and production planners,

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:48.442
and lower level management,
we were much less exposed

00:20:48.442 --> 00:20:49.400
to the memory business.

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:52.720
We had gradually rationalize
our factory infrastructure

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:54.308
so that we still
have to cut back.

00:20:54.308 --> 00:20:55.850
We still have to
shut down factories,

00:20:55.850 --> 00:21:00.080
but much less than had these
Cassandras not taking matters

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:01.620
into their own hands.

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:04.910
So spontaneous actions through
front line or lower level

00:21:04.910 --> 00:21:07.070
employees who are much
closer to the action

00:21:07.070 --> 00:21:12.230
is a very key element in causing
adjustment in the business

00:21:12.230 --> 00:21:14.090
structure of a company.

00:21:14.090 --> 00:21:16.700
That's a key element.

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:21.260
What tends to happen is when--

00:21:21.260 --> 00:21:23.090
I'm sorry, I took
this off too early.

00:21:23.090 --> 00:21:27.230
What tends to happen is there
is, at times like these,

00:21:27.230 --> 00:21:29.410
there's a growing divergence.

00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:31.970
A dissonance between
the actions of people

00:21:31.970 --> 00:21:34.940
who are close to the actions
and the comprehension

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:40.040
and statements of senior
management, like myself.

00:21:40.040 --> 00:21:44.450
I call that strategic dissonance
because a corporation,

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:46.220
in its daily
action, is beginning

00:21:46.220 --> 00:21:52.520
to do something deviant from
the stated mission, values,

00:21:52.520 --> 00:21:55.980
competencies, that
continues to get articulated

00:21:55.980 --> 00:21:56.730
in the management.

00:21:56.730 --> 00:22:02.420
So one of the big telltale
signs in a business that it

00:22:02.420 --> 00:22:04.430
is struggling with a
strategic inflection point

00:22:04.430 --> 00:22:07.520
is this growing and
seemingly irreconcilable

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:10.280
difference between
what the company does

00:22:10.280 --> 00:22:12.770
and what the company says
it's supposed to be doing.

00:22:15.620 --> 00:22:19.850
It's not because people
involved are dumb, or phony,

00:22:19.850 --> 00:22:21.740
or hypocritical, or
anything like that,

00:22:21.740 --> 00:22:23.280
but because senior
management was

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:24.655
responsible for
the articulation.

00:22:24.655 --> 00:22:26.960
It was giving the interview
that the employees

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:29.450
read in the newspaper.

00:22:29.450 --> 00:22:30.950
Doesn't know it yet.

00:22:30.950 --> 00:22:32.600
The employees know
it's not real.

00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:35.680
The senior management
doesn't know it's real.

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:37.640
That's a terribly strong sign.

00:22:37.640 --> 00:22:39.740
And I'm not going to
name names because it

00:22:39.740 --> 00:22:42.390
gets you a little
closer to present time.

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:46.120
But there's very
relevant examples

00:22:46.120 --> 00:22:49.760
of that taking place in the
recent past in the information

00:22:49.760 --> 00:22:52.500
technology industries.

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:57.080
To cope with it, to
cope with this process

00:22:57.080 --> 00:23:00.440
requires fundamental changes
in your business outlook.

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:02.240
Fundamental changes
in the definition

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:06.770
of what your business
is and what it might be.

00:23:06.770 --> 00:23:10.970
Fundamental changes in the core
competencies, the capabilities,

00:23:10.970 --> 00:23:12.920
of the corporation.

00:23:12.920 --> 00:23:15.600
If you've been doing this all
along, like in Intel's case.

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:18.560
We had been in the
microprocessor business

00:23:18.560 --> 00:23:20.682
for 10 years before
this change occurred.

00:23:20.682 --> 00:23:21.890
We had a lot of capabilities.

00:23:21.890 --> 00:23:26.000
We had the option of abandoning
our traditional business

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:29.690
and emphasizing an
emerging business.

00:23:29.690 --> 00:23:36.230
If we didn't, we would have been
up the creek without a paddle.

00:23:36.230 --> 00:23:39.560
It is very important,
for that reason alone,

00:23:39.560 --> 00:23:44.150
to cultivate additional
and corollary supporting

00:23:44.150 --> 00:23:49.190
competencies so that
when shifts take place,

00:23:49.190 --> 00:23:52.070
you've got places to shift to.

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:54.380
More about that
in a few minutes.

00:23:54.380 --> 00:23:58.130
A key issue that we
need to deal with

00:23:58.130 --> 00:24:03.807
is organizations,
corporations, individuals

00:24:03.807 --> 00:24:04.890
dealing with the carriers.

00:24:04.890 --> 00:24:10.020
Governments are constantly
surrounded by indications

00:24:10.020 --> 00:24:12.880
of monumental changes.

00:24:12.880 --> 00:24:14.210
And one of the--

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:17.700
they are not all real things.

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:21.180
For every real inflection
point causing change

00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:25.620
that you have to deal
with there are 10 items

00:24:25.620 --> 00:24:27.810
that are really not
much more than noise

00:24:27.810 --> 00:24:29.290
when you look back
retrospectively.

00:24:29.290 --> 00:24:32.760
So, how do you tell
one from the other?

00:24:32.760 --> 00:24:37.120
And I wish I was able to give
you a little diskette that you

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:41.390
run this program and fill in
the blanks and it will tell you.

00:24:41.390 --> 00:24:41.890
I can't.

00:24:44.850 --> 00:24:48.030
I have looked at and thought
through a variety of examples

00:24:48.030 --> 00:24:48.730
of this.

00:24:48.730 --> 00:24:56.040
And some examples may mean
something to some of you.

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:59.220
Ten, twelve years ago,
there was a big raging trend

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:03.330
toward X-ray lithography
in our industry.

00:25:03.330 --> 00:25:06.060
And particularly our
Japanese competitors

00:25:06.060 --> 00:25:07.560
were investing very
heavily into it.

00:25:07.560 --> 00:25:12.600
And some Americans felt that
unless we made that change

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:15.460
in the language that
I'm using today,

00:25:15.460 --> 00:25:18.930
we're going to go down
the hill and decline

00:25:18.930 --> 00:25:20.840
in a strategic inflection point.

00:25:20.840 --> 00:25:22.590
We analyzed this seven
ways through Sunday

00:25:22.590 --> 00:25:25.530
and decided it was
really a spurious noise.

00:25:25.530 --> 00:25:27.750
Again, using today's language.

00:25:27.750 --> 00:25:29.940
And we turned out to be right.

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:33.780
X-ray lithography in the last
10 years hasn't gone anywhere.

00:25:33.780 --> 00:25:36.030
Visible light and ultraviolet
light, lithography,

00:25:36.030 --> 00:25:38.550
doesn't matter what, the
conventional techniques

00:25:38.550 --> 00:25:40.530
could be extended much more.

00:25:40.530 --> 00:25:42.730
And we were right.

00:25:42.730 --> 00:25:44.580
And an interesting
question, does that

00:25:44.580 --> 00:25:48.730
mean that X-ray lithography
will never go anywhere?

00:25:48.730 --> 00:25:50.020
It doesn't.

00:25:50.020 --> 00:25:53.550
X-ray lithography
as a threat may

00:25:53.550 --> 00:25:58.150
have been a bit of noise
ten, twelve years ago.

00:25:58.150 --> 00:26:01.200
And may become a strategic
inflection point a year

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:04.120
from now, five years from
now, ten years from now.

00:26:04.120 --> 00:26:07.560
So there is not a general
deal that a certain phenomenon

00:26:07.560 --> 00:26:08.970
is right or wrong.

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:11.440
It is or is not a
strategic inflection point.

00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:14.490
It is in the context
of the time, context

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:16.770
of the state of the
business, the context

00:26:16.770 --> 00:26:20.605
of the evolution
of the technology.

00:26:20.605 --> 00:26:22.230
Another example that
I want to touch on

00:26:22.230 --> 00:26:27.140
is risk technology,
as in microprocessors.

00:26:27.140 --> 00:26:31.380
That was a late '80s phenomenon
and it was taken very seriously

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:36.240
by a lot of players as a
10x force, a 10x better

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:37.830
way of building
microprocessors that

00:26:37.830 --> 00:26:41.130
could in fact uproot the
structure of the business

00:26:41.130 --> 00:26:45.600
and create a lot of trouble for
established players like Intel.

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:47.430
And in fact, a lot
of people at Intel

00:26:47.430 --> 00:26:50.020
thought that might be the case.

00:26:50.020 --> 00:26:52.620
So we had enormous
raging debate.

00:26:52.620 --> 00:26:54.540
And a major
development, to hedge

00:26:54.540 --> 00:26:59.040
our bets of a risk
processor whose advocates

00:26:59.040 --> 00:27:02.070
and whose designers
believed it was a 10x force.

00:27:02.070 --> 00:27:06.460
And we continue to pursue our
regular standard architecture,

00:27:06.460 --> 00:27:10.860
whose designers and
developers thought it wasn't.

00:27:10.860 --> 00:27:14.040
So this debate took place
very heavily internally.

00:27:14.040 --> 00:27:15.840
And it was a very
expensive proposition

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:20.550
both in terms of double
investment streams

00:27:20.550 --> 00:27:22.170
and in terms of
the wear and tear

00:27:22.170 --> 00:27:24.645
on people in our management,
including myself,

00:27:24.645 --> 00:27:27.760
because I didn't understand the
issues involved here at all.

00:27:27.760 --> 00:27:30.450
This was way outside of
my technical competence.

00:27:30.450 --> 00:27:35.650
And I had to rely on the advice
of people, who all of a sudden

00:27:35.650 --> 00:27:37.300
barely said good
morning to each other.

00:27:37.300 --> 00:27:39.240
They were so emotional
on the subject.

00:27:41.890 --> 00:27:46.620
Now, we pursued this parallel
development up to a point,

00:27:46.620 --> 00:27:48.510
and then eventually
out of all this debate,

00:27:48.510 --> 00:27:51.720
and debate is in fact one of the
most important ways of dealing

00:27:51.720 --> 00:27:53.490
with this thing, we
came to the conclusion

00:27:53.490 --> 00:27:55.870
it was not a strategic
inflection point.

00:27:55.870 --> 00:28:00.550
That more, almost literally,
it was not a 10x improvement,

00:28:00.550 --> 00:28:02.880
it was a 2 to 3x improvement.

00:28:02.880 --> 00:28:07.020
And is likely to be less
and less as time goes on.

00:28:07.020 --> 00:28:11.040
Turns out, again in the
time frame we were right.

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:13.560
But before you were
right, half the people

00:28:13.560 --> 00:28:17.210
who were involved in the risk
process have left the company.

00:28:17.210 --> 00:28:21.180
The relationships that were
torn up, for the largest part,

00:28:21.180 --> 00:28:22.170
never were healed.

00:28:22.170 --> 00:28:25.470
So these things are not
fun and easy processes.

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:26.640
Even inside a company.

00:28:26.640 --> 00:28:28.560
Even among people who
know each other well.

00:28:34.410 --> 00:28:37.850
I'm going to switch to
a different subject,

00:28:37.850 --> 00:28:41.340
so we'll have opportunity
to do questions.

00:28:41.340 --> 00:28:46.680
Which is, this is what I dealt
with is methods of recognition

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:49.442
is a phenomenon of strategic
inflection point or not.

00:28:49.442 --> 00:28:51.150
But let's suppose we
are past that stage.

00:28:51.150 --> 00:28:54.090
That we are entering a
strategic inflection point.

00:28:54.090 --> 00:28:54.780
Dean Urban.

00:28:54.780 --> 00:28:55.363
Where are you?

00:28:55.363 --> 00:28:56.220
I see you.

00:28:56.220 --> 00:29:01.120
Is it proper for a lecturer
to remove his jacket?

00:29:01.120 --> 00:29:02.600
Thank you.

00:29:02.600 --> 00:29:05.610
URBAN: The audience [INAUDIBLE]

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:08.550
GROVE: No, I'm not going to
get that naked in a school.

00:29:16.780 --> 00:29:18.610
So, you are in one of them.

00:29:18.610 --> 00:29:22.870
You are in that valley of
the Death, Valley of death.

00:29:22.870 --> 00:29:26.980
And the subject I want
to talk for a few minutes

00:29:26.980 --> 00:29:28.540
is what are some
of the techniques

00:29:28.540 --> 00:29:31.390
that you can do
to get through it.

00:29:31.390 --> 00:29:34.090
And the best way
to look at that is,

00:29:34.090 --> 00:29:36.560
this process has two stages.

00:29:36.560 --> 00:29:38.960
The first half and
the second half.

00:29:38.960 --> 00:29:41.530
The first half, is
again keep in mind

00:29:41.530 --> 00:29:44.500
this morphing metaphor,
as you enter it

00:29:44.500 --> 00:29:47.710
from an orderly to a
disorderly state of affairs.

00:29:47.710 --> 00:29:51.460
From a phase that you recognize
to a bunch of mixed up bits.

00:29:51.460 --> 00:29:54.845
And things are
terribly confusing.

00:29:54.845 --> 00:29:56.470
The people, the
Cassandras that come in

00:29:56.470 --> 00:29:58.750
bring you the stories
from the field.

00:29:58.750 --> 00:30:01.150
From the [INAUDIBLE]
don't make any sense.

00:30:01.150 --> 00:30:03.500
You don't feel
comfortable anymore.

00:30:03.500 --> 00:30:09.420
So what you need to do is
let loose of the reins of it.

00:30:09.420 --> 00:30:11.650
Let chaos reign in your
organization about it.

00:30:11.650 --> 00:30:15.770
Let experimentation take place.

00:30:15.770 --> 00:30:22.000
Now, the natural state of
organizations is chaos.

00:30:22.000 --> 00:30:24.910
That applies to
all organizations.

00:30:24.910 --> 00:30:27.640
It's a corollary of the
second law of thermodynamics.

00:30:31.220 --> 00:30:34.540
So ordinarily
management is there

00:30:34.540 --> 00:30:39.640
to fight the dispersion of
the forces in the company

00:30:39.640 --> 00:30:42.140
and bring some
cohesion in there.

00:30:42.140 --> 00:30:44.920
So this is not a natural
act for management,

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:46.810
letting chaos reign.

00:30:46.810 --> 00:30:48.848
Particularly not a natural
act when management

00:30:48.848 --> 00:30:50.890
is getting a little
uncomfortable about the state

00:30:50.890 --> 00:30:51.967
of the business.

00:30:51.967 --> 00:30:53.800
And I'm sitting there
watching these things.

00:30:53.800 --> 00:30:55.290
I'm not that comfortable.

00:30:55.290 --> 00:30:56.900
So what is my
reaction going to be?

00:30:56.900 --> 00:31:00.670
I want to pull in the
reins, not let go.

00:31:00.670 --> 00:31:05.650
And in reality, in this
stage, we have to constantly,

00:31:05.650 --> 00:31:08.350
we have to discipline
ourselves to letting things go.

00:31:08.350 --> 00:31:11.560
Because the only way we
are going to put some shape

00:31:11.560 --> 00:31:15.730
and certainty into this
ambiguity in our business model

00:31:15.730 --> 00:31:18.410
is to let a bunch
of things go on

00:31:18.410 --> 00:31:20.010
and to watch them like a hawk.

00:31:20.010 --> 00:31:22.030
Now, I'm not going to
do the experiments.

00:31:22.030 --> 00:31:26.020
But I can very easily
watch all those experiments

00:31:26.020 --> 00:31:28.560
and see which one gets
traction and which one--

00:31:28.560 --> 00:31:32.080
I mean, I don't actually
mean technical experiments,

00:31:32.080 --> 00:31:35.620
although sometimes that might
be the answer to the question.

00:31:35.620 --> 00:31:37.330
But funding development
programs that

00:31:37.330 --> 00:31:39.820
are mutually contradictory.

00:31:39.820 --> 00:31:43.570
And you're going to get a lot
of stuff, backtalk, on this one.

00:31:43.570 --> 00:31:46.890
How can you give money for this
and not give money for that?

00:31:46.890 --> 00:31:49.150
And you are all confused.

00:31:49.150 --> 00:31:51.660
You have your head up in the
wrong place, et cetera, et

00:31:51.660 --> 00:31:53.810
cetera et cetera.

00:31:53.810 --> 00:31:56.440
But you have to bear with
that because at this point

00:31:56.440 --> 00:31:58.670
you simply are not in
a position to know.

00:31:58.670 --> 00:32:01.840
So this is the phase when
I say let chaos reign.

00:32:01.840 --> 00:32:03.910
Do a lot of experimentation.

00:32:03.910 --> 00:32:08.110
And one, I'm not covering
every item on this force so

00:32:08.110 --> 00:32:12.610
try not to stay synchronous
with the fall there.

00:32:12.610 --> 00:32:17.980
But what is extremely
important, ideally,

00:32:17.980 --> 00:32:20.980
is to start doing this
experimentation at a time

00:32:20.980 --> 00:32:24.310
when your existing
business is strong.

00:32:24.310 --> 00:32:28.022
It provides a protective
bubble over the totality

00:32:28.022 --> 00:32:28.730
of your business.

00:32:28.730 --> 00:32:32.275
Now this is so damn
logical it's not funny.

00:32:32.275 --> 00:32:37.060
The time to start playing
with new things, a time

00:32:37.060 --> 00:32:42.825
to start experimenting,
the time to start investing

00:32:42.825 --> 00:32:44.950
in those new things, to
develop those competencies,

00:32:44.950 --> 00:32:46.450
is when your core
business is wrong.

00:32:46.450 --> 00:32:48.580
And what is real life?

00:32:48.580 --> 00:32:50.220
Real life is I want to do that.

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:52.990
I'm going go to my best guy
here and say, OK, you're

00:32:52.990 --> 00:32:55.570
going to go on this
emerging stuff.

00:32:55.570 --> 00:32:58.315
And every general manager
is going to jump on me,

00:32:58.315 --> 00:33:00.190
how can you pull him
out of the business that

00:33:00.190 --> 00:33:01.480
pays all of our salaries?

00:33:01.480 --> 00:33:03.290
He says, OK, no, no,
no, I was kidding.

00:33:03.290 --> 00:33:03.670
I was kidding.

00:33:03.670 --> 00:33:04.420
It was a bad idea.

00:33:04.420 --> 00:33:05.650
Bad idea.

00:33:05.650 --> 00:33:07.510
Let me take him.

00:33:07.510 --> 00:33:10.720
And he came up way below him,
the other guy in the ranking.

00:33:10.720 --> 00:33:12.878
Let him do the new business.

00:33:12.878 --> 00:33:14.170
And he is really not very good.

00:33:14.170 --> 00:33:16.200
We are only keeping him
for tradition's sake.

00:33:19.210 --> 00:33:22.180
Nothing personal.

00:33:22.180 --> 00:33:23.680
Then we put him on the new area.

00:33:23.680 --> 00:33:27.820
And guess what, the new area
is not going to succeed.

00:33:27.820 --> 00:33:31.380
Now, is it not going to
succeed because it was

00:33:31.380 --> 00:33:32.630
a bad idea in the first place?

00:33:32.630 --> 00:33:36.400
Or is it not going to succeed
because we put our second class

00:33:36.400 --> 00:33:39.670
people on developing it?

00:33:39.670 --> 00:33:41.440
That's how it goes in real life.

00:33:41.440 --> 00:33:46.180
And you know, on the other hand
I keep coming back to that,

00:33:46.180 --> 00:33:47.370
management is not--

00:33:47.370 --> 00:33:49.120
you don't get drafted
to be a manager.

00:33:49.120 --> 00:33:51.380
You enlist to be a manager.

00:33:51.380 --> 00:33:54.050
You like all the
various elements of it.

00:33:54.050 --> 00:33:57.040
One flip side of it is
you have to do things

00:33:57.040 --> 00:33:58.330
that require self-discipline.

00:33:58.330 --> 00:34:01.060
It's kind of like dieting and
exercise and a bunch of stuff

00:34:01.060 --> 00:34:02.660
that you don't
necessarily want to do,

00:34:02.660 --> 00:34:04.210
but you do because
they're good for you.

00:34:04.210 --> 00:34:05.760
Making choices like
that, you've got

00:34:05.760 --> 00:34:09.310
to do because it comes
with the territory.

00:34:09.310 --> 00:34:12.010
Having said that,
very few people

00:34:12.010 --> 00:34:14.330
use the business bubble
to make their choices.

00:34:14.330 --> 00:34:15.969
They hang onto the
business bubble.

00:34:15.969 --> 00:34:18.070
They want to extend it.

00:34:18.070 --> 00:34:21.940
And they under invest in the
experimentation with the result

00:34:21.940 --> 00:34:28.100
that when things happen,
their wrong people

00:34:28.100 --> 00:34:29.330
are in the wrong territory.

00:34:29.330 --> 00:34:31.159
I'm not talking about them.

00:34:31.159 --> 00:34:33.800
I'm talking about me.

00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:34.699
Literally.

00:34:34.699 --> 00:34:37.580
In that situation when
we were in the DRAM

00:34:37.580 --> 00:34:39.080
business and the
production planners

00:34:39.080 --> 00:34:41.090
very cleverly over
some period of time

00:34:41.090 --> 00:34:44.270
got us out of DRAM
production and ramped up

00:34:44.270 --> 00:34:46.415
microprocessor production.

00:34:46.415 --> 00:34:48.290
That was good because
the production planners

00:34:48.290 --> 00:34:48.830
could do it.

00:34:48.830 --> 00:34:52.400
But only senior
management could assign

00:34:52.400 --> 00:34:55.940
development resources,
development laboratories,

00:34:55.940 --> 00:34:58.640
to do certain lines of work.

00:34:58.640 --> 00:35:00.710
So our best
laboratory was working

00:35:00.710 --> 00:35:02.520
on what, microprocessors?

00:35:02.520 --> 00:35:04.990
No, DRAMs.

00:35:04.990 --> 00:35:07.150
And they were doing
a phenomenal job.

00:35:07.150 --> 00:35:09.830
They were developing the
world's first seamless

00:35:09.830 --> 00:35:12.430
DRAM faster than anybody
else was, including

00:35:12.430 --> 00:35:14.210
the Japanese manufacturers.

00:35:14.210 --> 00:35:17.260
And it was completely
useless because we

00:35:17.260 --> 00:35:20.830
didn't have any factories in
the works to produce them.

00:35:20.830 --> 00:35:22.300
We didn't have
any real intention

00:35:22.300 --> 00:35:23.620
to put them into production.

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:25.810
In the meanwhile
the product that we

00:35:25.810 --> 00:35:28.630
were starving from development
resources for exactly

00:35:28.630 --> 00:35:31.210
the little exercise
that I played out,

00:35:31.210 --> 00:35:33.340
was the second
generation version

00:35:33.340 --> 00:35:36.670
of the 386, which happened
to be the stuff that

00:35:36.670 --> 00:35:40.530
led to those spectacular results
that Dean Urban described

00:35:40.530 --> 00:35:41.860
in the introduction.

00:35:41.860 --> 00:35:44.920
So you know, you don't have to
go very far to see examples.

00:35:44.920 --> 00:35:46.430
And we corrected it.

00:35:46.430 --> 00:35:49.960
But we corrected it,
kind of my universe

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:52.213
concept of my own
career, every decision

00:35:52.213 --> 00:35:54.130
that I've ever ended up
making that turned out

00:35:54.130 --> 00:35:57.550
to be a right decision, I made
it a minimum of a year late.

00:35:57.550 --> 00:36:01.540
This one we probably made
more like a year and a half

00:36:01.540 --> 00:36:03.100
to two years late.

00:36:03.100 --> 00:36:07.930
We took that theme and put them
on the second generation 386.

00:36:07.930 --> 00:36:09.940
And we did and fortunately
we were still ahead

00:36:09.940 --> 00:36:12.610
of the competition so
that we did OK with it.

00:36:12.610 --> 00:36:15.410
But in a more demanding
business environment,

00:36:15.410 --> 00:36:19.030
it would have been
too little too late.

00:36:19.030 --> 00:36:21.601
Then comes the second phase.

00:36:21.601 --> 00:36:25.540
The morphing process continues,
the bits are beginning to gel,

00:36:25.540 --> 00:36:29.480
and you begin to see the
outlines of the new picture.

00:36:29.480 --> 00:36:31.453
And at this point,
now you can do what

00:36:31.453 --> 00:36:32.620
you have been wanting to do.

00:36:32.620 --> 00:36:34.810
Pull in the reins.

00:36:34.810 --> 00:36:39.430
At this point, it's time to take
stock of those experimentation

00:36:39.430 --> 00:36:43.960
and make order and decide
what we are going to do

00:36:43.960 --> 00:36:47.820
is this and that.

00:36:47.820 --> 00:36:50.290
And interestingly,
by now your people

00:36:50.290 --> 00:36:53.590
may have gotten used to chaos,
and the kind of like chaos.

00:36:53.590 --> 00:36:56.240
Like I tell you,
organizations like chaos.

00:36:56.240 --> 00:37:02.020
So this is not exactly a
highly popular act either

00:37:02.020 --> 00:37:03.400
at the time it comes.

00:37:03.400 --> 00:37:07.140
The only difference is that if
you are like most companies,

00:37:07.140 --> 00:37:10.330
that you're not doing this in
the middle of a benign business

00:37:10.330 --> 00:37:13.430
bubble, but you are doing
it where you are in trouble.

00:37:13.430 --> 00:37:16.300
People will support
harsh actions

00:37:16.300 --> 00:37:21.550
because they want leadership
to drag them out of the mess

00:37:21.550 --> 00:37:24.140
that they are in.

00:37:24.140 --> 00:37:26.310
The catch is, you
know, the reason

00:37:26.310 --> 00:37:28.560
I have two hands is so
I can do on the one hand

00:37:28.560 --> 00:37:32.220
on the other hand, constantly.

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:34.500
On the other hand
on this one, is

00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:37.830
that you, the leader
of that organization,

00:37:37.830 --> 00:37:44.330
or you, the leader of your
own career, are not that sure.

00:37:44.330 --> 00:37:47.500
You're beginning to see that
picture gel as you're moving

00:37:47.500 --> 00:37:50.110
across the morphing process.

00:37:50.110 --> 00:37:52.210
But you're not very sure.

00:37:52.210 --> 00:37:55.000
But you have to
act as if you were.

00:37:55.000 --> 00:37:59.250
So you have to, you're
pulling the reins

00:37:59.250 --> 00:38:01.410
and commit yourself
to of course of action

00:38:01.410 --> 00:38:05.160
at a time when your
knowledge and your certainty

00:38:05.160 --> 00:38:06.720
lags way behind where you are.

00:38:11.860 --> 00:38:13.840
It's a very difficult process.

00:38:13.840 --> 00:38:15.410
A very imperfect process.

00:38:15.410 --> 00:38:19.490
It's a process that is
fraught with dangers

00:38:19.490 --> 00:38:22.630
because by committing
yourself to that,

00:38:22.630 --> 00:38:24.950
you're committing
yourself wrong.

00:38:24.950 --> 00:38:27.960
You are sunk.

00:38:27.960 --> 00:38:33.450
But, if you don't commit
yourself you are sunk for sure.

00:38:33.450 --> 00:38:36.200
Because in most of
these situations

00:38:36.200 --> 00:38:39.720
you are living in a
competitive environment

00:38:39.720 --> 00:38:43.540
where there's very little
tolerance for error.

00:38:43.540 --> 00:38:45.810
But if you're not
committing yourself,

00:38:45.810 --> 00:38:47.640
you're not going
to commit an error,

00:38:47.640 --> 00:38:53.250
you simply are giving
time to your competition.

00:38:53.250 --> 00:38:56.260
And there's kind of a
universal law in business.

00:38:56.260 --> 00:38:59.750
The only thing there
is in business is time.

00:38:59.750 --> 00:39:04.450
You give time to somebody else,
you take it away from yourself.

00:39:04.450 --> 00:39:07.510
That's all you got.

00:39:07.510 --> 00:39:13.090
So hesitation transfers
time to your competition.

00:39:13.090 --> 00:39:15.250
And that time, it
never will come back.

00:39:15.250 --> 00:39:18.190
So the earliest moment
make a work up your courage

00:39:18.190 --> 00:39:20.500
to commit yourself to
the reigning in the chaos

00:39:20.500 --> 00:39:23.880
is the time to act.

00:39:23.880 --> 00:39:26.310
I've talked about a
bunch of things here,

00:39:26.310 --> 00:39:34.080
and some of those things have
a very psychobabbly tone to it.

00:39:34.080 --> 00:39:39.240
And that's how it is.

00:39:39.240 --> 00:39:41.970
Managers are people.

00:39:41.970 --> 00:39:44.250
Their employees are people.

00:39:44.250 --> 00:39:51.630
And work, and living, and their
careers, and their long term

00:39:51.630 --> 00:39:53.970
success is very
important to each

00:39:53.970 --> 00:39:56.410
of these individuals involved.

00:39:56.410 --> 00:40:00.060
So when things go bad you're
going through the same stages

00:40:00.060 --> 00:40:02.610
as you go through in
personal catastrophes.

00:40:02.610 --> 00:40:05.470
You have to go through the
usual stages of denial,

00:40:05.470 --> 00:40:06.670
which is very, very strong.

00:40:06.670 --> 00:40:10.950
That is why you want to
shoot the Cassandras,

00:40:10.950 --> 00:40:14.345
because your denial of this
new thing that you don't want

00:40:14.345 --> 00:40:16.470
to deal with, that you
don't know how to deal with,

00:40:16.470 --> 00:40:18.810
is so strong.

00:40:18.810 --> 00:40:22.290
You work your way
through escapism.

00:40:22.290 --> 00:40:25.890
I have this deep
seeded suspicion

00:40:25.890 --> 00:40:30.000
that the predominant
reason people engage

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:34.560
in big time acquisitions
is to escape

00:40:34.560 --> 00:40:36.720
from dealing with a
strategic inflection

00:40:36.720 --> 00:40:37.950
point in their own business.

00:40:37.950 --> 00:40:40.010
In their own core business.

00:40:40.010 --> 00:40:45.483
Because nothing is as seductive
as teeing up and flying

00:40:45.483 --> 00:40:46.650
from one place to the other.

00:40:46.650 --> 00:40:50.100
Staying up all night with your
lawyers and your investment

00:40:50.100 --> 00:40:51.930
bankers and calling
a press conference

00:40:51.930 --> 00:40:54.780
and being on the front page
of the newspaper the next day.

00:40:54.780 --> 00:40:56.580
How does that compare
with figuring out

00:40:56.580 --> 00:40:59.421
which factory to shut down?

00:40:59.421 --> 00:41:03.150
I mean, which one
would you rather do?

00:41:03.150 --> 00:41:08.370
And it is so tempting to immerse
yourself into stuff like that

00:41:08.370 --> 00:41:10.230
as a legitimate escape.

00:41:10.230 --> 00:41:14.440
Not an escape like I go on the
golf course or go on a safari

00:41:14.440 --> 00:41:15.970
to Africa, but a
legitimate escape.

00:41:15.970 --> 00:41:18.450
I'm working real
hard to do that.

00:41:20.982 --> 00:41:22.190
I don't know if this is true.

00:41:22.190 --> 00:41:23.480
I don't know how to study it.

00:41:23.480 --> 00:41:25.610
But I do have that suspicion.

00:41:25.610 --> 00:41:28.040
But when you get out of this,
and the escape didn't work,

00:41:28.040 --> 00:41:31.670
you move on to the acceptance
and pertinent action.

00:41:31.670 --> 00:41:36.710
And the question here is,
do you do it in time enough,

00:41:36.710 --> 00:41:42.020
or do you wait until you have
frittered away your resources?

00:41:42.020 --> 00:41:46.040
And to a very large
extent, this is

00:41:46.040 --> 00:41:50.950
an issue of human makeup
as much as anything else.

00:41:50.950 --> 00:41:57.170
The last point I want to make,
everything I have said here

00:41:57.170 --> 00:41:58.850
applies to your careers.

00:41:58.850 --> 00:42:04.940
And I'm not talking to you as
a bunch of CEOs of corporations

00:42:04.940 --> 00:42:05.480
now.

00:42:05.480 --> 00:42:07.190
I'm talking to you
as a bunch of CEOs

00:42:07.190 --> 00:42:09.520
of your own little business.

00:42:09.520 --> 00:42:15.260
Starts at the top of your
head and ends at your toes.

00:42:15.260 --> 00:42:16.400
That's your corporations.

00:42:16.400 --> 00:42:17.805
You've been investing in it.

00:42:17.805 --> 00:42:18.930
Heaven knows, I don't know.

00:42:18.930 --> 00:42:20.370
Probably $30,000 a year.

00:42:20.370 --> 00:42:23.700
Whatever this
outfit charges here.

00:42:23.700 --> 00:42:26.630
And before, and you're
going to continue too.

00:42:26.630 --> 00:42:29.660
And it's yours to manage.

00:42:29.660 --> 00:42:33.470
And we are in a
very different era

00:42:33.470 --> 00:42:41.740
than my generation
managed career was in.

00:42:41.740 --> 00:42:43.990
My generation could
assume that when

00:42:43.990 --> 00:42:47.140
we went to work
for an enterprise,

00:42:47.140 --> 00:42:50.620
that enterprise could provide
us with a measure of stability

00:42:50.620 --> 00:42:53.410
that would guide us through,
in a very smooth fashion,

00:42:53.410 --> 00:42:55.180
the inflection point
that always came.

00:42:55.180 --> 00:42:55.900
That's not new.

00:42:55.900 --> 00:42:58.420
It was always around.

00:42:58.420 --> 00:43:00.730
Enterprises are now going
through inflection points

00:43:00.730 --> 00:43:07.630
of their own that doesn't
guarantee their own survival.

00:43:07.630 --> 00:43:09.635
So enterprises are
far more preoccupied

00:43:09.635 --> 00:43:11.260
with their own survival
today than they

00:43:11.260 --> 00:43:14.240
were a generation ago.

00:43:14.240 --> 00:43:18.250
And therefore, they are less
able physically less able

00:43:18.250 --> 00:43:21.640
and emotionally and interest
wise and concentration

00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:24.310
wise, less able to
deal with the survival

00:43:24.310 --> 00:43:27.040
of the many little careers,
the many little businesses

00:43:27.040 --> 00:43:28.678
that their employees represent.

00:43:31.960 --> 00:43:35.200
This subtle favorite
cliches of business life

00:43:35.200 --> 00:43:39.490
today make this problem worse.

00:43:39.490 --> 00:43:42.530
We love globalization.

00:43:42.530 --> 00:43:45.700
Globalization means that
the world is our oyster.

00:43:45.700 --> 00:43:50.890
We can sell a product
everywhere from here to Jindong.

00:43:50.890 --> 00:43:56.230
Globalization also means that
every person, every working

00:43:56.230 --> 00:44:01.870
person of the same general
training, same general business

00:44:01.870 --> 00:44:05.450
occupation in the world
competes with you.

00:44:05.450 --> 00:44:09.610
That they work for companies
that compete for your company.

00:44:09.610 --> 00:44:17.430
In a brief period of 30 years,
from the '60s to the '90s,

00:44:17.430 --> 00:44:21.100
the United States
in the aggregate

00:44:21.100 --> 00:44:27.250
has gone from an almost
dominant industrial power

00:44:27.250 --> 00:44:31.840
to one of the leading
industrial powers.

00:44:31.840 --> 00:44:35.800
If you look at that
as a market analysis,

00:44:35.800 --> 00:44:39.210
being a dominant
industrial power,

00:44:39.210 --> 00:44:40.870
a dominant power
in your industry

00:44:40.870 --> 00:44:44.470
versus being one of the
contenders back into the Porter

00:44:44.470 --> 00:44:49.480
model dealing with substitution,
dealing with new entrants,

00:44:49.480 --> 00:44:54.520
dealing with intense
intra-industry rivalry, life

00:44:54.520 --> 00:44:58.900
has gotten a lot tougher for
the United States economy.

00:44:58.900 --> 00:45:00.982
Consequently, life has
gotten a lot tougher

00:45:00.982 --> 00:45:03.190
for companies that make up
the United States economy.

00:45:03.190 --> 00:45:05.570
Consequently, those companies
are much less able to.

00:45:05.570 --> 00:45:08.900
It's not that they became venal,
not that they became evil.

00:45:08.900 --> 00:45:11.140
They're simply less
able to provide

00:45:11.140 --> 00:45:16.480
this style of career development
and career maintenance

00:45:16.480 --> 00:45:17.760
that they used to do it.

00:45:17.760 --> 00:45:21.930
Now if they don't do it,
who's going to do it?

00:45:21.930 --> 00:45:24.580
And I'm advocating
basically doing

00:45:24.580 --> 00:45:27.430
the same concept of
strategic analysis

00:45:27.430 --> 00:45:30.160
and apply it to your career.

00:45:30.160 --> 00:45:36.450
And foremost among those is
watch for strategic inflection

00:45:36.450 --> 00:45:36.950
points.

00:45:39.480 --> 00:45:43.460
Work on separating the
signal from the noise.

00:45:43.460 --> 00:45:48.020
And we complete, conclude
that it's signal.

00:45:48.020 --> 00:45:51.080
Experiment first and
then pull in the reins

00:45:51.080 --> 00:45:53.470
and commit yourself.

00:45:53.470 --> 00:45:56.170
And discipline
yourself to overcome

00:45:56.170 --> 00:46:00.430
the denial and the various
other human foibles that

00:46:00.430 --> 00:46:03.430
will come in the way
in your own career,

00:46:03.430 --> 00:46:06.350
as well as in your business.

00:46:06.350 --> 00:46:08.480
So, that's all I have,
and I will take questions

00:46:08.480 --> 00:46:09.760
from both here and outside.

