00:56:32.040 --> 00:56:35.490
Use personal
computing and enhance

00:56:35.490 --> 00:56:39.810
the use of personal computing
in communications applications.

00:56:39.810 --> 00:56:44.100
And do so in such a way that
we can make a business out

00:56:44.100 --> 00:56:49.350
of providing that
kind of application.

00:56:49.350 --> 00:56:54.690
Yesterday we had a major
symposium in New York City that

00:56:54.690 --> 00:56:58.040
was called Wired for
Management Initiative,

00:56:58.040 --> 00:56:59.235
or an Intel symposium.

00:56:59.235 --> 00:57:03.630
We filled the Madison Square
Garden conference center, not

00:57:03.630 --> 00:57:06.570
the boxing arena.

00:57:06.570 --> 00:57:11.160
Over 3,000 people came
up for a daylong set

00:57:11.160 --> 00:57:13.560
of technical presentation
and demonstrations

00:57:13.560 --> 00:57:19.230
whose element was basically
gelling all of these expertise

00:57:19.230 --> 00:57:24.840
to deliver servicing of
client, computer clients,

00:57:24.840 --> 00:57:27.990
from in a client server,
[? PCBS ?] client server

00:57:27.990 --> 00:57:31.020
environment, through a
remote on the wire, down

00:57:31.020 --> 00:57:33.593
the wire, management capability.

00:57:33.593 --> 00:57:35.760
We have become, this is not
a gigantic business yet.

00:57:35.760 --> 00:57:38.370
But I think it is
a decent business.

00:57:38.370 --> 00:57:39.480
A growing business.

00:57:39.480 --> 00:57:43.530
And a very important business
for letting the world

00:57:43.530 --> 00:57:47.160
progress toward this notion
of communicating applications.

00:57:47.160 --> 00:57:52.290
We are the world's leader in
management software, group

00:57:52.290 --> 00:57:54.630
level management
software applications.

00:57:54.630 --> 00:57:56.670
Not well-known, I mean
better known today

00:57:56.670 --> 00:57:58.720
than it was yesterday.

00:57:58.720 --> 00:58:01.050
But it is a result of that.

00:58:01.050 --> 00:58:03.210
So, we are experimenting.

00:58:03.210 --> 00:58:05.280
We are building our
core competencies.

00:58:05.280 --> 00:58:09.990
And we have in fact moved
people along my little skit,

00:58:09.990 --> 00:58:13.350
into that field who have
been very proficient leaders

00:58:13.350 --> 00:58:14.350
in microprocessors.

00:58:14.350 --> 00:58:16.740
So, will it all work?

00:58:16.740 --> 00:58:17.790
Will it be significant?

00:58:17.790 --> 00:58:20.470
Was our bet right?

00:58:20.470 --> 00:58:23.672
We'll know that
in 10 years time.

00:58:23.672 --> 00:58:24.648
Yeah, please.

00:58:29.050 --> 00:58:31.890
AUDIENCE: I was wondering,
can you hear me?

00:58:31.890 --> 00:58:32.430
GROVE: Yeah.

00:58:32.430 --> 00:58:33.570
AUDIENCE: I was
wondering if you think

00:58:33.570 --> 00:58:35.190
that there might be
such thing as too

00:58:35.190 --> 00:58:37.170
much control over an industry.

00:58:37.170 --> 00:58:42.030
What I'm trying to
understand is that, you all

00:58:42.030 --> 00:58:44.490
have a very big influence
over the industry

00:58:44.490 --> 00:58:46.320
that you're working in.

00:58:46.320 --> 00:58:51.360
And as such, you're controlling
a lot of the value chain.

00:58:51.360 --> 00:58:54.270
And I'm wondering
is, in the long term,

00:58:54.270 --> 00:58:58.230
if you feel responsible for more
than your particular company,

00:58:58.230 --> 00:59:00.610
which is huge and
a lot of employees.

00:59:00.610 --> 00:59:03.150
But I'm wondering
how you, if you,

00:59:03.150 --> 00:59:06.540
feel responsible for the
company beyond, for the industry

00:59:06.540 --> 00:59:07.930
beyond, your company.

00:59:07.930 --> 00:59:13.662
And if so, what kind of
actions do you take to do that?

00:59:13.662 --> 00:59:15.120
GROVE: A very
interesting question.

00:59:19.380 --> 00:59:23.310
Let me start with, you kind of
segue through several questions

00:59:23.310 --> 00:59:27.440
here, but let me try
with one of them.

00:59:27.440 --> 00:59:29.790
What do we feel responsible for?

00:59:29.790 --> 00:59:33.360
Which is a key question.

00:59:33.360 --> 00:59:37.540
We have a very healthy share
of the microprocessor market.

00:59:37.540 --> 00:59:41.100
One thing that's clear for us is
that we cannot grow a whole lot

00:59:41.100 --> 00:59:44.170
more by increasing
that market share.

00:59:44.170 --> 00:59:49.050
Some place in the last several
years, our corporate priority

00:59:49.050 --> 00:59:52.380
has changed in a
very significant way

00:59:52.380 --> 00:59:55.440
to growing the personal
computing industry.

00:59:58.230 --> 01:00:00.690
Not growing our share of the
personal computing industry,

01:00:00.690 --> 01:00:03.180
but undertaking
initiatives which

01:00:03.180 --> 01:00:05.080
may or may not turn
into businesses.

01:00:05.080 --> 01:00:06.830
The one I just described
being an example.

01:00:06.830 --> 01:00:08.330
If it turns into a
business it's not

01:00:08.330 --> 01:00:10.295
going to be a gigantic
business, but maybe it

01:00:10.295 --> 01:00:11.670
won't turn into
a business but it

01:00:11.670 --> 01:00:13.095
will grow the whole industry.

01:00:15.600 --> 01:00:19.950
In that sense, we are working
on behalf of the entire industry

01:00:19.950 --> 01:00:24.160
because our entire focus
is growth, growth, growth.

01:00:26.700 --> 01:00:31.440
In the process of doing
that we have adopted

01:00:31.440 --> 01:00:34.440
a certain set of internal
rules and regulations

01:00:34.440 --> 01:00:38.910
which in the simplest form is
being extremely open about what

01:00:38.910 --> 01:00:42.870
we do and not closing
up specifications,

01:00:42.870 --> 01:00:48.010
except when it comes to the very
crown jewels of our company,

01:00:48.010 --> 01:00:50.317
which is microprocessor design.

01:00:50.317 --> 01:00:51.900
Other than that all
of our initiatives

01:00:51.900 --> 01:00:53.888
are industry wide initiatives.

01:00:53.888 --> 01:00:55.430
Now having said
that, let me tell you

01:00:55.430 --> 01:00:58.230
who I feel the
biggest obligation to,

01:00:58.230 --> 01:01:00.360
and that's consistent
with this drive.

01:01:00.360 --> 01:01:02.670
I feel our biggest
obligation is not

01:01:02.670 --> 01:01:06.330
to the members of the
industry, but to the members

01:01:06.330 --> 01:01:11.040
of the larger community of
information technology users

01:01:11.040 --> 01:01:13.790
who really determine
whether PCs are in

01:01:13.790 --> 01:01:16.350
or PCs are out, or
think clients are

01:01:16.350 --> 01:01:19.000
in or think clients are
out, whether PCs have

01:01:19.000 --> 01:01:21.480
become boring or not boring.

01:01:21.480 --> 01:01:27.060
We all have a very,
very strong user focus.

01:01:27.060 --> 01:01:31.570
And we study user communities
in terms of consumers,

01:01:31.570 --> 01:01:34.230
small businesses, large
businesses, internationally,

01:01:34.230 --> 01:01:35.320
constantly.

01:01:35.320 --> 01:01:39.240
We have various formal dialogues
with representative bodies

01:01:39.240 --> 01:01:40.170
of them.

01:01:40.170 --> 01:01:41.880
We merchandise to them.

01:01:41.880 --> 01:01:45.000
And every once in a while
when we run into trouble we'll

01:01:45.000 --> 01:01:45.695
find--

01:01:45.695 --> 01:01:47.865
there's a Pentium
story, we end up

01:01:47.865 --> 01:01:50.490
having to service them directly,
even though we didn't actually

01:01:50.490 --> 01:01:51.730
sell anything to them.

01:01:51.730 --> 01:01:54.150
So the answer to your
question, yes, we

01:01:54.150 --> 01:01:55.830
feel responsible
for the industry.

01:01:55.830 --> 01:01:58.288
Not because we are such good
guys, but because the only way

01:01:58.288 --> 01:02:00.480
we can grow is if
the industry grows.

01:02:00.480 --> 01:02:04.290
And the way we intend to
discharge this responsibility

01:02:04.290 --> 01:02:07.200
is constant focus
on the evolving

01:02:07.200 --> 01:02:11.282
needs or potential needs
of the user community.

01:02:18.230 --> 01:02:20.170
AUDIENCE: Hello.

01:02:20.170 --> 01:02:24.460
You spoke earlier today about
complementary or complementors

01:02:24.460 --> 01:02:27.580
as one of the six forces in
that five forces diagram.

01:02:27.580 --> 01:02:30.040
And I think we all understand
how Microsoft fits in

01:02:30.040 --> 01:02:33.610
with Intel and how that
complements Intel's business.

01:02:33.610 --> 01:02:37.750
My question is, I'm curious
how your joint development

01:02:37.750 --> 01:02:40.420
agreements with Hewlett-Packard,
how that's going to fit in.

01:02:40.420 --> 01:02:43.990
And looking into the future how
Intel and Hewlett-Packard are

01:02:43.990 --> 01:02:47.600
going to be able to
complement each other.

01:02:47.600 --> 01:02:52.030
GROVE: I don't think that fits
the category of complimentor.

01:02:52.030 --> 01:02:55.270
Not all business
relationships are

01:02:55.270 --> 01:03:02.230
complementors in this sense of
two capabilities complementing

01:03:02.230 --> 01:03:04.450
each other in the context
of the industry structure.

01:03:04.450 --> 01:03:06.033
What we are doing
with Hewlett-Packard

01:03:06.033 --> 01:03:10.420
is a business arrangement
where we got some technology

01:03:10.420 --> 01:03:15.100
from them and they are going
to receive in various forms

01:03:15.100 --> 01:03:18.640
that I'm not at liberty
to discuss payback

01:03:18.640 --> 01:03:20.410
for that technology.

01:03:20.410 --> 01:03:22.480
And out of that will
come another generation

01:03:22.480 --> 01:03:23.390
of microprocessor.

01:03:23.390 --> 01:03:25.510
The general extension of
our microprocessor line

01:03:25.510 --> 01:03:28.480
to 64-bit width.

01:03:28.480 --> 01:03:30.980
That is going to be an
industry standard product.

01:03:30.980 --> 01:03:33.820
So this is more a
kind of a mixing

01:03:33.820 --> 01:03:39.040
of two organizations genetic
pools, technically speaking.

01:03:39.040 --> 01:03:41.020
And it's a quid pro
quo, an arrangement

01:03:41.020 --> 01:03:44.320
between two companies.

01:03:44.320 --> 01:03:46.900
When the dust settles,
Hewlett-Packard

01:03:46.900 --> 01:03:51.190
will be a computer manufacturer
and will use Intel microchips

01:03:51.190 --> 01:03:52.890
just like it does today.

01:03:52.890 --> 01:03:56.320
Probably in a more
extensive fashion

01:03:56.320 --> 01:03:58.720
as we transition to 64 bits.

01:03:58.720 --> 01:04:00.400
And those
microprocessors will be

01:04:00.400 --> 01:04:03.530
better for them and for
the rest of the industry

01:04:03.530 --> 01:04:06.610
and for the users back in
the previous questions,

01:04:06.610 --> 01:04:09.220
because they put that
technology in there.

01:04:09.220 --> 01:04:11.920
Now, HP thought
about the technology

01:04:11.920 --> 01:04:16.090
long before they put
that on the table.

01:04:16.090 --> 01:04:21.070
And they realized that
technology's value is only

01:04:21.070 --> 01:04:23.710
as good as the spread
of that technology

01:04:23.710 --> 01:04:24.880
through the industry.

01:04:24.880 --> 01:04:27.650
That they could not really
specialize and make it,

01:04:27.650 --> 01:04:29.860
turn it into
commercial value given

01:04:29.860 --> 01:04:32.290
that in the personal
computing area,

01:04:32.290 --> 01:04:33.790
or in the computing
area altogether,

01:04:33.790 --> 01:04:37.240
they have a relatively small
share of the total market.

01:04:37.240 --> 01:04:39.100
So they made the
conscious decision

01:04:39.100 --> 01:04:41.350
to adopt to this
horizontal open industry

01:04:41.350 --> 01:04:44.310
model with their
technology through us.

01:04:44.310 --> 01:04:48.790
And we become a distributor
of the particular part

01:04:48.790 --> 01:04:51.430
of the technology that we
incorporate into our products

01:04:51.430 --> 01:04:52.970
for the rest of the industry.

01:04:52.970 --> 01:04:56.780
And then they negotiated
something in return for that.

01:04:56.780 --> 01:04:58.330
So I don't think
it's going to change

01:04:58.330 --> 01:04:59.455
the nature of the industry.

01:04:59.455 --> 01:05:02.740
I think it is going
to however, result

01:05:02.740 --> 01:05:05.470
in a better 64-bit
microprocessor generation

01:05:05.470 --> 01:05:09.140
that we ourselves, just
with our own genetic pool,

01:05:09.140 --> 01:05:12.013
could have been
able to generate.

01:05:12.013 --> 01:05:13.055
URBAN: OK, last question.

01:05:13.055 --> 01:05:14.860
This Is from an undergraduate.

01:05:14.860 --> 01:05:16.540
Given what you know
now, what advice

01:05:16.540 --> 01:05:20.000
would you give yourself in
1968 about running and building

01:05:20.000 --> 01:05:20.500
Intel?

01:05:36.960 --> 01:05:39.960
GROVE: On a personal
basis, I can comment.

01:05:39.960 --> 01:05:43.400
On a corporate basis
I really can't.

01:05:43.400 --> 01:05:45.232
Because I don't,
you know you got

01:05:45.232 --> 01:05:46.440
to put this into perspective.

01:05:46.440 --> 01:05:49.620
I was 32 years old
when I went to Intel.

01:05:49.620 --> 01:05:51.030
I was a research engineer.

01:05:51.030 --> 01:05:53.610
I never went near a
production, had never

01:05:53.610 --> 01:05:55.878
gone near a production
line and the like.

01:05:55.878 --> 01:05:57.420
My biggest worry
when I went to Intel

01:05:57.420 --> 01:06:01.550
was I really didn't know how
I'm going to earn my keep.

01:06:01.550 --> 01:06:03.870
And I mean this in
all seriousness.

01:06:03.870 --> 01:06:08.700
So I adapted to the situation.

01:06:08.700 --> 01:06:09.225
I did OK.

01:06:13.587 --> 01:06:16.380
But I think in the early
stages I was somewhat

01:06:16.380 --> 01:06:24.140
hampered by this question, what
is a research guy like me going

01:06:24.140 --> 01:06:27.780
to do in a commercial startup?

01:06:27.780 --> 01:06:31.080
So I don't know if
that's a general advice

01:06:31.080 --> 01:06:32.790
or general problem
or general advice,

01:06:32.790 --> 01:06:35.260
but to myself, I would
say don't worry about it.

01:06:35.260 --> 01:06:37.070
It's going to work out OK.

01:06:37.070 --> 01:06:38.100
It did.

01:06:42.280 --> 01:06:46.090
As to how Intel could have done
better in those early days,

01:06:46.090 --> 01:06:49.490
we started out extremely well.

01:06:49.490 --> 01:06:52.930
We hit recessions and
various bumps and grinds.

01:06:52.930 --> 01:06:55.180
But strategically, we
focused on one thing, which

01:06:55.180 --> 01:06:57.010
was semiconductor memories.

01:06:57.010 --> 01:07:00.430
We started much in the
same kind of hedged

01:07:00.430 --> 01:07:03.105
our technological bets
and went for a while

01:07:03.105 --> 01:07:04.550
in three different directions.

01:07:04.550 --> 01:07:05.980
We jettisoned one pretty early.

01:07:05.980 --> 01:07:07.990
Jettisoned the
second one after we

01:07:07.990 --> 01:07:11.230
saw that MOS memories worked.

01:07:11.230 --> 01:07:16.120
Jettisoned bipolar
memories a few years later.

01:07:16.120 --> 01:07:20.140
So, with the first few
years strategically

01:07:20.140 --> 01:07:23.950
I can't see, even in retrospect,
what we could have done better.

01:07:23.950 --> 01:07:29.990
Later, we started getting
trapped, which is a big lesson.

01:07:29.990 --> 01:07:33.670
Getting trapped in our own
success and our own legacy.

01:07:33.670 --> 01:07:40.300
And were slower to
change with opportunities

01:07:40.300 --> 01:07:43.500
and responding to the
threats than we might have.

01:07:43.500 --> 01:07:46.125
But that's what this
whole thing was about.

01:07:46.125 --> 01:07:47.500
So thank you very
much for coming

01:07:47.500 --> 01:07:48.750
and thanks for your questions.

01:07:48.750 --> 01:07:52.540
[APPLAUSE]

01:07:52.540 --> 01:07:57.940
URBAN: Just as a
final announcement.

01:07:57.940 --> 01:08:00.520
Dr. Grove's book is
actually on sale outside.

01:08:00.520 --> 01:08:02.120
And since my background
is marketing,

01:08:02.120 --> 01:08:04.390
he promised me a Commission
for every book you sell.

01:08:04.390 --> 01:08:06.790
It's the greatest
book you'll ever get.

01:08:06.790 --> 01:08:10.390
And our next lecture
will be on December 3rd.

01:08:10.390 --> 01:08:13.840
Mr. DeSimone, who is the
CEO of 3M corporation.

01:08:13.840 --> 01:08:17.460
So continuing a
great tradition here.

